Review round 3: bundled review launches

Purpose
Workers should run one bundled script for each reviewer or fixer round instead
of rebuilding runtime commands. The launcher owns date/model selection, safe
frontmatter passing, fresh task files and explicit launch failures. Both ship
briefs and scout promotions carry the same launcher instructions and policy.

Current outcome
All five earlier P2 findings are now held by the fresh round-3 reviewer.
No new defect was reproduced. The captain asked to stop at this boundary,
so no final approval review or additional fix round has started.
Implementation fixes are committed at 144b2486cbd400478def16c0020c9e11924aa026.
This summary is an additional commit; a later final review must cover that head.
No review-completion receipt or done event has been recorded.

History and approved work
Round 1 found diagnostics/blank text accepted as a reply (R1-1), lost policy
when commands were guessed from text (R1-2), valid dotted task names refused
(R1-3), and tests missing duplicate model launches (R1-4). It also asked for
an explicit task-specific fixer handoff destination (Q1), and raised a
large-policy performance question (Q2).
Firstmate approved all four fixes and Q1; Q2 remains excluded. The first
fix round separated replies from diagnostics, strengthened output validation,
used shared task-name validation, counted launches, and named a NEW handoff
file inside each task directory. Those changes passed their targeted checks.

Round 2 held those original fixes except R1-2: the full legacy launch command
still survived as a competing instruction. It also found R2-1: stream-wise
Claude JSON filtering accepted success followed by an error. For example:
  {"type":"result","is_error":false,"result":"reply"}
  {"type":"result","is_error":true,"result":"failed"}
The old launcher returned success for both roles despite the second object.

The captain approved a structural correction for R1-2 and exactly-one-result
validation for R2-1, with red tests first. The second fixer recorded causes
before editing production code. All three affected test files failed against
the pre-fix implementation; the launcher accepted ten invalid complete
responses, and both generators lacked a structural launch boundary.

The approved structural correction removed command recognition entirely.
One exact original-config launch marker is filled by the shared renderer.
Existing configurations without a marker receive an automatic launch slot.
Every surrounding policy fragment is retained inside bounded policy blocks;
configured launch commands remain inert examples, while policy requirements
remain binding. The generated slot alone directs role launches. This preserves
the full legacy text without guessing which lines to delete. Duplicate slots
refuse before either generator writes instructions. Literal replacements are
not rescanned, and embedded fences cannot escape their enclosing policy block.
The renderer header is the authoritative contract; documentation points to it.

Claude now validates complete stdout as exactly one expected result object,
requires is_error=false and a string result, then rejects blank replies.
Both roles share this path. Multiple success/error objects, unrelated objects,
arrays, scalars and malformed/trailing bytes return nonzero with preserved
raw stdout and diagnostics. A single valid object with surrounding whitespace
passes. No persistent coordination state was introduced.

Round 3 independently re-ran old reproductions and targeted tests.
R1-1, R1-2, R1-3, R1-4, R2-1 and approved Q1 were all held.
The success/error example above now returns exit 1 for reviewer and fixer.
Legacy and first/middle/last-slot templates preserve their policy correctly.
Twelve independent mutations were detected, with zero survivors; files were
restored byte-for-byte and the final working tree remained clean.

Open findings and questions
No open P1/P2, no new minor finding and no reproduced regression.
Q2: large-policy performance remains explicitly deferred and unprobed.
Its current likelihood/impact is unknown; it is not demonstrated as a new
regression. Resuming a stress investigation would expand this approved scope.
Q3: actual live Claude receipts and a worker's interpretation of the generated
policy boundary remain unobserved. Deterministic fakes verify the executable
contract; a real Claude receipt or dispatched-worker exercise could provide
additional evidence, but no such call is included or silently authorized here.
Any practical impact remains uncertain rather than a reproduced defect.

